To ensure BGP automatically shuts down a neighbor connection when BFD fails, use the `bfd-enable` command under the BGP neighbor or group configuration. This ties the BGP session state directly to the BFD session state:

```cli
configure router
    bgp
        group <group-name>
            neighbor <neighbor-ip>
                bfd-enable
                exit
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- The `bfd-enable` command forces BGP to inherit its operational state (up/down) from the BFD session. If BFD fails, BGP immediately tears down the neighbor connection.  
- Ensure BFD parameters (multiplier, intervals) are also configured under the relevant IP interface to guarantee BFD sessions are established.  

**Note:** Replace `<group-name>` and `<neighbor-ip>` with your specific BGP group and neighbor IP address.
