```console
configure router
    autonomous-system 65001
    bgp
        local-as 65100 private no-prepend-global-as
        group "SUBBGP-65100"
            peer-as 65100
            family ipv4
            neighbor 10.0.0.1
        exit
        group "SUBBGP-65101"
            peer-as 65101
            family ipv4
            neighbor 10.0.1.1
        exit
        group "SUBBGP-65102"
            peer-as 65102
            family ipv4
            neighbor 10.0.2.1
        exit
        group "EXTERNAL-BGP"
            family ipv4
            neighbor 203.0.113.5
                peer-as 65005
            exit
        exit
    exit
exit
```

**Explanation:**  
- The global AS `65001` is set at the router level using `autonomous-system 65001`.  
- The local sub-AS `65100` is configured under BGP with `local-as 65100 private no-prepend-global-as`:  
  - `private` hides the sub-AS in paths learned from peers.  
  - `no-prepend-global-as` suppresses prepending the global AS when advertising to peers.  
- Internal sub-AS peerings use distinct groups (`SUBBGP-65100`, `SUBBGP-65101`, `SUBBGP-65102`) with their respective `peer-as` values.  
- External BGP peers (e.g., AS `65005`) are configured in a separate group with their own `peer-as`.  
- `family ipv4` enables IPv4 routing for all peer groups.  

Replace neighbor IPs (e.g., `10.0.0.1`, `203.0.113.5`) with actual peer addresses. Repeat neighbor entries for all required peers in each group.
