To adjust how the router selects tunnels for forwarding labeled BGP routes, configure the next-hop resolution settings under the labeled routes transport-tunnel context. Use the following CLI commands:

```cli
configure router bgp
    next-hop-resolution
        labeled-routes
            transport-tunnel
                family [vpn-ipv4 | label-ipv4 | label-ipv6]
                    resolution [any | filter | disabled]
                    resolution-filter
                        allow-ldp
                        allow-rsvp
                        allow-sr-te
                        allow-sr-bsd
                    exit
                exit
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

### Key parameters:
- **`family`**: Specify the route type (e.g., `vpn-ipv4` for VPN routes, `label-ipv4`/`label-ipv6` for labeled unicast).
- **`resolution`**:  
  - `any`: Use any tunnel type (default TTM preference).  
  - `filter`: Restrict to tunnels explicitly allowed under `resolution-filter`.  
  - `disabled`: Disable tunnel resolution for this route family.  
- **`resolution-filter`**: Define permitted tunnel types (e.g., LDP, RSVP, SR-TE, SR-BSD).

### Example for VPN-IPv4 routes:
```cli
configure router bgp
    next-hop-resolution
        labeled-routes
            transport-tunnel
                family vpn-ipv4
                    resolution filter
                    resolution-filter
                        allow-rsvp
                        allow-sr-te
                    exit
                exit
            exit
        exit
    exit
```

This configuration forces VPN-IPv4 routes to use only RSVP or SR-TE tunnels. Adjust the `family` and tunnel types (`allow-ldp`, `allow-rsvp`, etc.) based on your route types and preferred path selection.
